{"id": "swebq0158", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 2.55, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1726, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Constitutional republic;Presidential system;Republic;Unitary state", "query": "what form of government do we have in the philippines?", "transcript": "What form of government do we have in the Philippines?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The Philippines has a presidential representative democracy. The President serves as both the head of state and head of government, elected through a direct popular vote for a six-year term. Legislative power is held by a bicameral Congress consisting of the Senate and the House of Representatives. The judiciary operates independently, interpreting laws and ensuring their constitutionality. This system combines elements of both presidential and parliamentary systems, with strong executive powers alongside a complex legislative structure.", "answer_ms": 2010}
