{"id": "swebq0218", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 3.9, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.285, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Presidential system;Federal republic;Representative democracy;Two-party system;Constitutional republic;Republic", "query": "what kind of government did the united states have after the revolution?", "transcript": "What kind of government did the United States have after the Revolution?", "mode": "local", "answer": "After the American Revolution, the United States adopted a form of government known as a **federal republic** with a **democratic framework**. This system combined elements of both federalism and representative democracy, and it was structured through a written constitution—the U.S. Constitution—which was ratified in 1788. The new government replaced the monarchy of the British Crown with a system where power was divided among three branches—legislative, executive, and judicial—to prevent any one group from gaining too much authority. This structure aimed to balance individual rights with national unity, laying the foundation for the modern democratic republic that exists today.", "answer_ms": 2945}
